<p class="eyebrow">WHAT THE MEDITERRANEAN WOMEN KNOW</p>
<h1 style="font-family:'Source Serif 4',Georgia,serif;font-weight:800;font-size:clamp(28px,7vw,40px);line-height:1.13;color:#1F2419">Why Some Women Eat Everything on Vacation and Still Feel Light: <span class="hl">7 Things They Do Differently</span></h1>
<p class="t" style="font-size:20px">On a 7-day cruise, almost everyone comes home puffy and heavier. Except the Greek and Italian women, who ate the bread, the pasta and the dessert, and still looked light. It isn’t genetics. It’s a few everyday habits, and one of them is two oils they’ve used their whole lives.</p>
<p style="font-size:13px;color:#5E6656">By the Resilia editorial team · Updated November 2026 · 3 min read</p>
<img src="{{ 't33-hero.jpg' | asset_url }}" alt="A woman on a cruise ship deck at sunset with her hand on her bloated belly" style="width:100%;aspect-ratio:4/3;object-fit:cover;border-radius:14px">
<section class="sec" style="border-top:0;padding-top:0">
<div class="sign"><div class="num">1</div><div><h3>They don’t skip the meal. They support the gut that digests it.</h3><p>Their bodies keep moving fluid and waste along, so a big dinner doesn’t sit in the face and belly by morning.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">2</div><div><h3>They use oregano like salt</h3><p>Wild oregano goes on the bread, the fish, the salad and the eggs. It’s been part of the Mediterranean kitchen for thousands of years.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">3</div><div><h3>Black seed lives in the pantry, not the medicine cabinet</h3><p>Across the Mediterranean and the Middle East, black seed has been an everyday food for generations.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">4</div><div><h3>They walk after dinner</h3><p>Walking is what keeps your lymphatic system moving. It has no pump of its own.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">5</div><div><h3>They don’t starve before the trip</h3><p>Starving and then feasting swings fluid back and forth. Steady is easier on your body.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">6</div><div><h3>They protect their sleep</h3><p>Deep sleep is when your body does much of its clearing.</p></div></div>
<div class="sign"><div class="num">7</div><div><h3>They don’t blame their genes, or their willpower</h3><p>It isn’t luck. It’s a routine. And the routine travels.</p></div></div>
<div class="box" style="text-align:center"><p style="font-size:19px;font-weight:800">Same food. Same week. Different body.</p><p class="t" style="font-size:17px">The difference isn’t what they skip. It’s what they support every day.</p></div>
</section>
<section class="sec"><h2 class="h2">Why some women puff up and others don’t</h2>
<img src="https://resilia.shop/cdn/shop/files/hf_20260803_191106_e4857c9a-536a-44b7-a4f9-d39a8ad4175a.png?v=1785785207&width=700" alt="Illustration of the lymphatic system" style="width:60%;margin:0 auto;aspect-ratio:3/4;object-fit:contain;border-radius:12px;background:#F7F9F4">
<p class="t">Your lymphatic system works alongside your bloodstream to move excess fluid, help clear waste and carry dietary fats from your gut.</p>
<p class="t">It has no pump of its own. It moves when you move, breathe deeply and sleep well.</p>
<p class="t">A week of travel slows it down: flights, bus rides and deck chairs, salty food, late nights. What should move along sits in the face, belly and ankles. That’s why two women can eat the same meal and only one of them wakes up puffy.</p>
<p style="font-size:12px;font-weight:900;letter-spacing:.12em;color:#3F5A1E;margin-top:4px">THE LOOP · EACH STEP FEEDS THE NEXT</p>
<div class="loop"><div>Travel days, salty food and late nights</div><div>You sit more and move less</div><div>Your body’s clean-up slows</div><div>Fluid builds in the face, belly and ankles</div><div>You feel puffy and heavy, so you move even less</div><div>The loop repeats</div></div></section>
